Labor displacement + future shock
MODEL  2026-09-10

TYPES
  Sector          : trucking | clerical | code | claims | care | trades
  Mile            : loaded long-haul interstate ton-mile
  Task            : atomic unit of work (drive, file, draft, exception)
  Job             : bundle of tasks + identity + wage
  ODD             : operational design domain (weather, map, road class)
  Tail            : work outside ODD (ice, yard, crash, customer site)
  OpsCenter       : remote-assist / AI corp dispatch (1:few -> 1:many)
  Shock           : social lag after economic share has already moved

VARIABLES
  A = share of MILES automated          // corridor story
  J = share of JOBS deleted             // not equal to A
  T = share of TASKS automated          // closest to "60%"
  W = weather/ODD coverage
  U = truck utilization hours/day       // human ~11; AV target ~20+
  R = remote-assist ratio               // humans per N trucks
  L = labor share of operating cost     // long-haul often 0.35-0.45
  D = demand for remaining human work
  S = public awareness                  // low until after pricing moves

FUNDAMENTALS
  1. "all" is rhetoric. remainder always exists.
  2. 85-15  = highway linehaul in reasonable weather
              85% miles AV, 15% tails human
  3. 60-40  = aggregate labor hit across sectors
              60% of routine TASKS, not 60% of every JOB
  4. tasks != jobs != miles
     A can be high while J stays moderate
  5. one sector is a story
     same pattern in parallel is a regime
  6. creep then recognition
     markets price A before institutions price J
  7. plateau claim ("search and replace")
     judges the present demo
  8. curve claim ("future shock")
     judges lag between working corridor and public map

CLAIMS IN THREAD
  JD: UBI inevitable as labor-share patch
      OTR easiest to imagine; bots later for fine control
      not all; 85-15 or 60-40; rough times
      shock arrives before you clock it
